MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Rework vsnprintf into a single conversion core (fmt_conv) fed by an argument
table, and add the pieces a conforming printf needs that were missing:

- POSIX positional arguments: %n$ for the conversion argument and *m$ for a
  '*' width/precision.  When any '$' appears the engine switches to a two-pass
  path (collect argument classes by index, read them, then format), otherwise
  it consumes va_arg sequentially as before.  This was a real bug: CDE's
  localized messages use them (dtsvc.msg msg 1 is "%1$s%2$s%3$s"), and the old
  printf rendered the literal format — the dterror dialog title came up as
  "X$sX$sX$s".  Now formats to "Workspace Manager: ...".
- '0' flag accepted in any order with the other flags (was only recognised in
  one position); '\'' and 'I' parsed as locale no-ops; BSD 'q' length = ll.
- Sign flags ('+'/' ') now apply to f/F/e/E/g/G with the sign placed before
  '0' fill (e.g. "%+08.1f" of 3.5 -> "+00003.5") — previously dropped.
- glibc %m (strerror(errno), no argument); %c honours field width; %hn/%hhn.
- Fix %zd/%zu: the size_t slot was read unsigned, so %zd of a negative value
  printed as a huge unsigned number.

All other conversions, flags, width/precision and the float/hex-float helpers
are unchanged.  New host test tests/lib/c/host/test_printf.c diffs substrate's
vsnprintf against the host libc over 83 directives (built -m32 to match the
i386 ABI): all pass.  lib/c builds clean under -Werror.

